helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156423				0	hr	Opening Microsoft Office Files by Default				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153092				0	hr	Converting Many Microsoft Office Files into OpenDocument Format				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3146986				0	hr	The \<emph\>Document Converter Wizard\</emph\> will copy and convert all Microsoft Office files in a folder into $[officename] documents in the OpenDocument file format. You can specify the folder to be read, and the folder where the converted files are to be saved.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3150486				0	hr	Choose \<link href=\"text/shared/autopi/01130000.xhp\" name=\"File - AutoPilot - Document Converter\"\>\<emph\>File - Wizards - Document Converter\</emph\>\</link\> to start the wizard.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154319				0	hr	Macros in Microsoft Office and $[officename]				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3154921				0	hr	Microsoft Office and $[officename] cannot run the same macro code. Microsoft Office uses VBA (Visual Basic for Applications) code, and $[officename] uses Basic code based on the $[officename] API (Application Program Interface) environment. Although the programming language is the same, the objects and methods are different.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id0804200804173539				0	hr	The most recent versions of %PRODUCTNAME can run some Excel Visual Basic scripts if you enable this feature at \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - Load/Save - VBA Properties\</item\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3152577				0	hr	If you use macros in one of the applications and want to use the same functionality in the other application, you must edit the macros. $[officename] can load the macros that are contained within Microsoft Office files and you can then view and edit the macro code in the $[officename] \<link href=\"text/shared/main0600.xhp\" name=\"Basic IDE\"\>Basic IDE\</link\> editor.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152596				0	hr	You can choose to preserve or delete VBA macros				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3153144				0	hr	Open a Microsoft Office document that contains VBA macro code. Change only the normal contents (text, cells, graphics), and do not edit the macros. Save the document as a Microsoft Office file type. Open the file in Microsoft Office, and the VBA macros will run as before.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3150011				0	hr	You may delete the VBA macros from the Microsoft Office file on loading or on saving.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3155366				0	hr	Choose \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01130100.xhp\" name=\"Tools - Options - Load/Save - VBA Properties\"\>\<emph\>Tools - Options - Load/Save - VBA Properties\</emph\>\</link\> to set the VBA macro handling of $[officename].				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\active_help_on_off.xhp	0	help	tit				0	hr	Turning Extended Tips On and Off				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\active_help_on_off.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156414				0	hr	\<bookmark_value\>Help; extended tips on/off\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>extended tips in Help\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>tips;extended tips in Help\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>tooltips;extended tips\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>activating;extended help tips\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\active_help_on_off.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156414				0	hr	\<variable id=\"active_help_on_off\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/active_help_on_off.xhp\" name=\"Turning Extended Tips On and Off\"\>Turning Extended Tips On and Off\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\active_help_on_off.xhp	0	help	par_id3157958				0	hr	\<emph\>Extended tips\</emph\> provide a brief description of the function of a particular icon, text box or menu command when you rest your cursor on that item.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\active_help_on_off.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155339				0	hr	To turn Extended Tips on and off:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\active_help_on_off.xhp	0	help	par_id3154823				0	hr	Choose \<emph\>Tools - Options - %PRODUCTNAME - General\</emph\>, and check \<emph\>Extended tips\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\active_help_on_off.xhp	0	help	par_id3149398				0	hr	A check mark indicates that the extended tips are activated.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\active_help_on_off.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149811				0	hr	To turn Extended Tips on temporarily:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\active_help_on_off.xhp	0	help	par_id3153541				0	hr	Press the shortcut keys Shift+F1 to activate extended tips once.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\active_help_on_off.xhp	0	help	par_id3153825				0	hr	A question mark appears beside the mouse pointer. You can move this \<emph\>Help Mouse Pointer\</emph\> over all controls, icons and menu commands to obtain a description of the command. The \<emph\>Help Mouse Pointer\</emph\> is disabled the next time you click the mouse.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\insert_specialchar.xhp	0	help	tit				0	hr	Inserting Special Characters				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\insert_specialchar.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154927				0	hr	\<bookmark_value\>characters; special\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting; special characters\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>special characters\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>text; inserting special characters\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>accents\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>compose key to insert special characters\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\insert_specialchar.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154927				0	hr	\<variable id=\"insert_specialchar\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/insert_specialchar.xhp\" name=\"Inserting Special Characters\"\>Inserting Special Characters\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\insert_specialchar.xhp	0	help	par_id3147576				0	hr	This function allows you to insert special characters, such as check marks, boxes, and telephone symbols, into your text.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\insert_specialchar.xhp	0	help	par_id3155535				0	hr	To view a selection of all characters, choose \<emph\>Insert - Special Character\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\insert_specialchar.xhp	0	help	par_id3147275				0	hr	In the large selection field click the desired character or several characters in succession. The characters are displayed at the bottom of the dialog. When you close the dialog with \<emph\>OK\</emph\>, all displayed characters in the selected font are inserted in the current document.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\insert_specialchar.xhp	0	help	par_id3153031				0	hr	In any text input field (such as the input fields in the \<emph\>Find&Replace\</emph\> dialog) you can press Shift+\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+S to call the \<emph\>Special Characters\</emph\> dialog.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\insert_specialchar.xhp	0	help	par_id3155630				0	hr	\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"UNIX\"\>At present there are three ways of entering letters with accents directly from the keyboard. \</caseinline\>\</switchinline\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\insert_specialchar.xhp	0	help	par_id3154897				0	hr	\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"UNIX\"\>\<emph\>Sun Solaris:\</emph\> Using a Sun keyboard. First press the Compose key to the right of the space bar, then enter the first and second modifiers. \</caseinline\>\</switchinline\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\insert_specialchar.xhp	0	help	par_id3145315				0	hr	\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"UNIX\"\>\<emph\>Linux / NetBSD:\</emph\> Using the dead-keys. In an xterm window first press the (´) or (`) key. The character should not appear on the screen. Now press a letter, such as "e". The e is given an accent, é or è. If not, then check in the XF86Config file if a "nodeadkeys" XkbdVariant has been loaded there and replace it. You may also have set the environment variable SAL_NO_DEADKEYS, which deactivates the dead-keys. \</caseinline\>\</switchinline\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\insert_specialchar.xhp	0	help	par_id3148943				0	hr	\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"UNIX\"\>\<emph\>All Unix systems:\</emph\> (Alt Graph) as additional compose key. The (Alt Graph) key can work in $[officename] like the Compose key, if you set the environment variable SAL_ALTGR_COMPOSE. The (Alt Graph) key must trigger a mode_switch, so, for example, xmodmap -e "keysym Alt_R = Mode_switch" must be set. First press (Alt Graph), then the first modifier, then the second modifier. The characters are combined as described on a Solaris system in the file /usr/openwin/include/X11/Suncompose.h. \</caseinline\>\</switchinline\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\insert_specialchar.xhp	0	help	par_id3149047				0	hr	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/04100000.xhp\" name=\"Special Characters\"\>Special Characters\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\insert_specialchar.xhp	0	help	par_id3153896				0	hr	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/06040200.xhp\" name=\"AutoCorrect\"\>AutoCorrect\</link\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	tit				0	hr	Scripting %PRODUCTNAME				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	bm_id5277565				0	hr	\<bookmark_value\>assigning scripts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Basic;scripting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>BeanShell scripting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Java;scripting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>programming;scripting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>scripting in programming\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>form controls;assigning macros\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>pictures;assigning macros\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>hyperlinks;assigning macros\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>shortcut keys;assigning macros\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1070A				0	hr	\<variable id=\"scripting\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/scripting.xhp\"\>Scripting %PRODUCTNAME\</link\>\</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10728				0	hr	You can assign custom scripts (macros) to menu items, icons, dialog controls, and events in %PRODUCTNAME.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1072B				0	hr	%PRODUCTNAME internally supports the following scripting languages:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10731				0	hr	%PRODUCTNAME Basic				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10739				0	hr	JavaScript				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1073D				0	hr	BeanShell				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_id6797082				0	hr	Python				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1091F				0	hr	In addition, developers can use high-level languages, for example Java programming language, to control %PRODUCTNAME externally. See the API project at OpenOffice.org.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10751				0	hr	To assign a script to a new menu entry				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10758				0	hr	Choose \<emph\>Tools - Customize\</emph\>, and click the \<emph\>Menus\</emph\> tab.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1093D				0	hr	Click \<emph\>Add\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10760				0	hr	In the \<emph\>Category\</emph\> list box, scroll down and open the "%PRODUCTNAME Macros" entry.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10768				0	hr	You see entries for "%PRODUCTNAME Macros" (scripts in the share directory of your %PRODUCTNAME installation), "My Macros" (scripts in the user directory), and the current document. Open any one of them to see the supported scripting languages.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1076C				0	hr	Open any scripting language entry to see the available scripts. Select a script.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10770				0	hr	A list of the script functions appears in the \<emph\>Functions\</emph\> list box. Select a function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10778				0	hr	Click \<emph\>New\</emph\> to create a new menu assignment. The new menu entry appears in the \<emph\>Menu entries\</emph\> list box. Click the menu entry and enter text to rename the entry.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10783				0	hr	To assign a script to a key combination				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10787				0	hr	Choose \<emph\>Tools - Customize - Keyboard\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A59				0	hr	In the \<emph\>Category\</emph\> list box, scroll down and open the "%PRODUCTNAME Macros" entry.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A61				0	hr	You see entries for "%PRODUCTNAME Macros" (scripts in the share directory of your %PRODUCTNAME installation), "My Macros" (scripts in the user directory), and the current document. Open any one of them to see the supported scripting languages.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A65				0	hr	Open any scripting language entry to see the available scripts. Select any script.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A69				0	hr	A list of the script functions will appear in the \<emph\>Functions\</emph\> list box. Select any function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A71				0	hr	Click the option button for %PRODUCTNAME or Writer (or whichever application is currently open). 				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A74				0	hr	Selecting the option button sets the scope of the new key combination to be applicable in all of %PRODUCTNAME or only in documents of the current module.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A78				0	hr	Select a key combination from the \<emph\>Shortcut keys\</emph\> list box and click \<emph\>Modify\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1078A				0	hr	To assign a script to an event				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1078E				0	hr	Choose \<emph\>Tools - Customize - Events\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A16				0	hr	Click \<emph\>Assign Macro\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10A9E				0	hr	In the \<emph\>Library\</emph\> list box, scroll down and open the "%PRODUCTNAME Macros" entry.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AA6				0	hr	You see entries for "%PRODUCTNAME Macros" (scripts in the share directory of your %PRODUCTNAME installation), "My Macros" (scripts in the user directory), and the current document. Open any one of them to see the supported scripting languages.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AAA				0	hr	Open any scripting language entry to see the available scripts. Select any script.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AAE				0	hr	A list of the script functions will appear in the \<emph\>Assigned Macros\</emph\> list box. Select any function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AB6				0	hr	Select to save in %PRODUCTNAME or current document.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AB9				0	hr	This sets the scope of the new event assignment to be applicable in all of %PRODUCTNAME or only in documents of the current module.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10ABD				0	hr	Select an event from the list and click \<emph\>Assign\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10791				0	hr	To assign a script to an event for an embedded object				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10795				0	hr	Select the embedded object, for example a chart, in your document.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10ADB				0	hr	Choose \<emph\>Format - Object - Macro\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10ADF				0	hr	In the \<emph\>Macros\</emph\> list box, open the %PRODUCTNAME Scripts entry.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AE7				0	hr	You see entries for share (scripts in the share directory of your %PRODUCTNAME installation), user (scripts in the user directory), and the current document. Open any one of them to see the supported scripting languages.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AEB				0	hr	Open any scripting language entry to see the available scripts. Select any script.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AEF				0	hr	A list of the script functions will appear in the \<emph\>Existing macros in\</emph\> list box. Select any function.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AF7				0	hr	Select an event from the list and click \<emph\>Assign\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10798				0	hr	To assign a script to a hyperlink				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1079C				0	hr	Position the cursor inside the hyperlink. 				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10B15				0	hr	Choose \<emph\>Insert - Hyperlink\</emph\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10B19				0	hr	Click the \<emph\>Events\</emph\> button.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN10B21				0	hr	Select and assign as stated above.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN1079F				0	hr	To assign a script to a graphic				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\scripting.xhp	0	help	par_idN107A3				0	hr	Select the graphic in your document. 				20181231 09:50:43
