helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150661				0	hr	Example: select * from Item where ItemName like 'The *%' {escape '*'}				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148541				0	hr	The example will give you all of the entries where the item name begins with 'The *'. This means that you can also search for characters that would otherwise be interpreted as placeholders, such as *, ?, _, % or the period.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150572				0	hr	\<emph\>Outer Join\</emph\> Escape Sequence: {oj outer-join}				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156052				0	hr	Example: select Article.* from {oj item LEFT OUTER JOIN orders ON item.no=orders.ANR}				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153674				0	hr	Querying text fields				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149134				0	hr	To query the content of a text field, you must put the expression between single quotes. The distinction between uppercase and lowercase letters depends on the database in use. LIKE, by definition, is case-sensitive (though some databases don't see it that strict).				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149302				0	hr	Querying date fields				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157998				0	hr	\<emph\>Date fields\</emph\> are represented as #Date# to clearly identify them as dates. Date, time and date/time constants (literals) used in conditions can be of either the SQL Escape Syntax type, or default SQL2 syntax.				20181231 09:50:43
