helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN10638				0	hr	You can use a JDBC driver to access an Oracle database from Solaris or Linux. To access the database from Windows, you need an ODBC driver.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN1064B				0	hr	On UNIX, ensure that the Oracle database client is installed with JDBC support. The JDBC driver class for the Solaris Oracle client version 8.x is located in the <Oracle client>/product/jdbc/lib/classes111.zip directory. You can also download the latest version from the Oracle web site:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN1064E				0	hr	http://otn.oracle.com/tech/java/sqlj_jdbc/content.html				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN10661				0	hr	In the \<emph\>Data source URL\</emph\> box, enter the location of the Oracle database server. The syntax of the URL depends on the database type. See the documentation that came with the JDBC driver for more information. 				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN10668				0	hr	For an Oracle database, the syntax of the URL is:				20181231 09:50:43
