helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149002	35			0	hr	\<emph\>Trials\</emph\> is the number of independent trials.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148875	36			0	hr	\<emph\>SP\</emph\> is the probability of success on each trial.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3145352	37			0	hr	\<emph\>T_1\</emph\> defines the lower limit for the number of trials.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149538	38			0	hr	\<emph\>T_2\</emph\> (optional) defines the upper limit for the number of trials.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148768				0	hr	Primjer				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154633				0	hr	What is the probability with ten throws of the dice, that a six will come up exactly twice? The probability of a six (or any other number) is 1/6. The following formula combines these factors:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149393	41			0	hr	=B(10; 1/6; 2) returns a probability of 29%.				20181231 09:50:43
