helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149282				0	hr	The formula in cell B16 is =DCOUNT(A1:E10;0;A13:E14)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150962				0	hr	Database Function Parameters:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155837				0	hr	The following are the parameter definitions for all database functions:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149453				0	hr	\<emph\>Database\</emph\> is the cell range defining the database.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3151272	86			0	hr	\<emph\>Database field\</emph\> specifies the column where the function operates on after the search criteria of the first parameter is applied and the data rows are selected. It is not related to the search criteria itself. Use the number 0 to specify the whole data range. \<variable id=\"quotes\"\>To reference a column by means of the column header name, place quotation marks around the header name. \</variable\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3147083				0	hr	\<emph\>Search criteria\</emph\> is the cell range containing search criteria. If you write several criteria in one row they are connected by AND. If you write the criteria in different rows they are connected by OR. Empty cells in the search criteria range will be ignored.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3151188				0	hr	Choose \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060500.xhp\" name=\"Tools - Options - Spreadsheet - Calculate\"\>Tools - Options - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - Calculate\</link\> to define how $[officename] Calc acts when searching for identical entries.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3882869				0	hr	See also the Wiki page about \<link href=\"https://wiki.openoffice.org/wiki/Documentation/How_Tos/Conditional_Counting_and_Summation\"\>Conditional Counting and Summation\</link\>.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150882				0	hr	\<bookmark_value\>DCOUNT function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>counting rows;with numeric values\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150882				0	hr	DCOUNT				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3156133				0	hr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBANZAHL\"\>DCOUNT counts the number of rows (records) in a database that match the specified search criteria and contain numerical values.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156099				0	hr	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153218				0	hr	DCOUNT(Database; Database Field; Search Criteria)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153273	187			0	hr	For the Database Field parameter you can enter a cell to specify the column, or enter the number 0 for the entire database. The parameter cannot be empty. \<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/01/04060101.xhp#quotes\"/\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154743				0	hr	Primjer				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153623				0	hr	In the example above (scroll up, please), we want to know how many children have to travel more than 600 meters to school. The result is to be stored in cell B16. Set the cursor in cell B16. Enter the formula =DCOUNT(A1:E10;0;A13:E14) in B16. The \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\> helps you to input ranges.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149142				0	hr	\<emph\>Database\</emph\> is the range of data to be evaluated, including its headers: in this case A1:E10. \<emph\>Database field\</emph\> specifies the column for the search criteria: in this case, the whole database. \<emph\>Search criteria\</emph\> is the range where you can enter the search parameters: in this case, A13:E14.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3145652				0	hr	To learn how many children in second grade are over 7 years of age, delete the entry >600 in cell D14 and enter "2" in cell B14 under Grade, and enter >7 in cell C14 to the right. The result is 2. Two children are in second grade and over 7 years of age. As both criteria are in the same row, they are connected by AND.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156123				0	hr	\<bookmark_value\>DCOUNTA function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>records;counting in Calc databases\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>counting rows;with numeric or alphanumeric values\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156123				0	hr	DCOUNTA				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3156110				0	hr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBANZAHL2\"\>DCOUNTA counts the number of rows (records) in a database that match the specified search conditions, and contain numeric or alphanumeric values.\</ahelp\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3143228				0	hr	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3146893				0	hr	DCOUNTA(Database; Database Field; Search Criteria)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149751				0	hr	Primjer				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153982				0	hr	In the example above (scroll up, please), you can search for the number of children whose name starts with an E or a subsequent letter. Edit the formula in B16 to read =DCOUNTA(A1:E10;"Age";A13:E14). Delete the old search criteria and enter >=E under \<emph\>Name\</emph\> in field A14. The result is 5. If you now delete all number values for Greta in row 8, the result changes to 4. Row 8 is no longer included in the count because it does not contain any values. The name Greta is text, not a value. Note that the \<item type=\"literal\"\>Database Field\</item\> parameter must point to a column that can contain values.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147256				0	hr	\<bookmark_value\>DGET function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell contents;searching in Calc databases\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>searching;cell contents in Calc databases\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147256				0	hr	DGET				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3152801				0	hr	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBAUSZUG\"\>DGET returns the contents of the referenced cell in a database which matches the specified search criteria.\</ahelp\> In case of an error, the function returns either #VALUE! for no row found, or Err502 for more than one cell found.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159344				0	hr	Syntax				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154696				0	hr	DGET(Database; Database Field; Search Criteria)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153909				0	hr	Primjer				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155388				0	hr	In the above example (scroll up, please), we want to determine what grade a child is in, whose name was entered in cell A14. The formula is entered in cell B16 and differs slightly from the earlier examples because only one column (one database field) can be entered for \<emph\>Database field\</emph\>. Enter the following formula:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153096				0	hr	=DGET(A1:E10;"Grade";A13:E14)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3150524				0	hr	Enter the name Frank in A14, and you see the result 2. Frank is in second grade. Enter "Age" instead of "Grade" and you will get Frank's age.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148833				0	hr	Or enter the value 11 in cell C14 only, and delete the other entries in this row. Edit the formula in B16 as follows:				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149912				0	hr	=DGET(A1:E10;"Name";A13:E14)				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148813				0	hr	Instead of the grade, the name is queried. The answer appears at once: Daniel is the only child aged 11.				20181231 09:50:43
