helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3144687				0	hr	Column A contains several X1 values, column B several X2 values and column C the Y values. You have already entered these values in your spreadsheet. You have now set up E2:G6 in the spreadsheet and activated the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>. For the LINEST function to work, you must have marked the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> check box in the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>. Next, select the following values in the spreadsheet (or enter them using the keyboard):				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158020				0	hr	\<emph\>Data_Y\</emph\> is C2:C8				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158039				0	hr	\<emph\>Data_X\</emph\> is A2:B8				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158058				0	hr	\<emph\>Linear_Type\</emph\> and \<emph\>Stats\</emph\> are both set to 1.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158084				0	hr	As soon as you click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>, $[officename] Calc will fill the above example with the LINEST values as shown in the example.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158106				0	hr	The formula in the \<emph\>Formula\</emph\> Bar corresponds to each cell of the LINEST array {=LINEST(C2:C8;A2:B8;1;1)}				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158128				0	hr	\<emph\>This represents the calculated LINEST values:\</emph\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3158146				0	hr	\<bookmark_value\>slopes, see also regression lines\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>regression lines\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158146				0	hr	E2 and F2: Slope \<emph\>m\</emph\> of the regression line y=b+m*x for the x1 and x2 values. The values are given in reverse order; that is, the slope for x2 in E2 and the slope for x1 in F2.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158184				0	hr	G2: Intersection b with the y axis.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3158204				0	hr	\<bookmark_value\>standard errors;array functions\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158204				0	hr	E3 and F3: The standard error of the slope value.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145845				0	hr	G3: The standard error of the intercept				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145859				0	hr	\<bookmark_value\>RSQ calculations\</bookmark_value\>				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145859				0	hr	E4: RSQ				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145880				0	hr	F4: The standard error of the regression calculated for the Y value.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145894				0	hr	E5: The F value from the variance analysis.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145915				0	hr	F5: The degrees of freedom from the variance analysis.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145937				0	hr	E6: The sum of the squared deviation of the estimated Y values from their linear mean.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145952				0	hr	F6: The sum of the squared deviation of the estimated Y value from the given Y values.				20181231 09:50:43
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN11B04				0	hr	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#moreontop\"/\>				20181231 09:50:43
